Lisp lambda function cache
=========================

Run from the build root, using each configured Lisp:

  timeout 180 ./maxima-local --no-init --lisp=sbcl \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=[rtest_parallel]);'

Require "No unexpected errors" and reject any "Maxima encountered a Lisp
error" message, even with exit status zero. The full core/share suite
includes these cases. Existing mathematical expected answers are unchanged.

LAMBDA-EXPR-FUN caches compiled Lisp lambda expressions for MAPPLY1. This
is separate from Maxima's own lambda evaluator. A cache lock protects both
lookups and the entire capacity/eviction/publication transaction, including
its private random state. Compilation stays outside the lock because a
compiler macro may call LAMBDA-EXPR-FUN recursively. A second lookup after
compilation decides whether insertion needs eviction. Every caller receives
its own coercion result, and the last publication remains in the cache;
reusing an inner compilation could change an outer call's result.
This permits duplicate compilation on concurrent misses; it does not claim
that arbitrary compiler callbacks or executed functions are thread safe.

The 29 appended test pairs load the helper, run 27 checks and clean up.
They check cache limits zero, one, four and 128, warm hits, distinct EQ keys,
input preservation, failed coercion, multiple values and optional/rest
arguments. Eviction RNG checks use already compiled functions: CLISP's
compiler itself uses the caller RNG, which is independent of cache eviction.

A deterministic generator supplies 64 exact signed integer cases, with
coefficients of 1 through 257 bits. MAPPLY1 invokes Maxima multiplication
and addition; expected answers use independent integer shift/subtraction.
Serial, caller, native worker, forced fallback and public scheduler paths
must execute their bodies and preserve the input expressions. No floating
point tolerance or simplification of old expected answers is involved.

A real Lisp macro supplies a compiler callback, without replacing COERCE
or LAMBDA-EXPR-FUN. Nested compilation exposes the old size overflow even
without threads when COERCE expands macros eagerly. COERCE only promises
a closure, so the fixture probes this behavior before checking compiler
callbacks. Interpreted coercion retains the cache/value checks; SBCL
also explicitly tests its interpreter mode. See the CLHS COERCE entry:
https://www.lispworks.com/documentation/HyperSpec/Body/f_coerce.htm .
GCL may apply recognized interpreted functions directly in MAPPLY1; its
arithmetic check allows that documented cache bypass. GCL is unverified
here. Two native compilers rendezvous on misses and publish in
a controlled order: cold/full cache bounds, both callers' own results and the last cached result
are checked. A serial same-key recursive compiler probe additionally preserves the outer
result, unrelated entries and eviction RNG. Four actual workers then stress
repeated eviction and shared hits. Worker identities, completion and exact results are asserted. Waits
have five-second deadlines; cleanup joins each worker once, including CCL.

Native probes run on threaded SBCL, CCL and ECL even when the public ECL
21.2.1 scheduler falls back to serial execution. Other configurations still
run the portable reentry, error and arithmetic checks. Run in an otherwise
idle test process: fixtures use isolated caches/RNGs and restore scheduler
counters. These tests do not certify other shared tables or asynchronous
interruption of a Lisp runtime's hash-table implementation.
